===============================================================================
Testing the ENETC Credit-Based Shaper (CBS / IEEE 802.1Qav) with a DPDK VF
===============================================================================

Overview
========
This guide describes how to exercise the ENETC transmit Credit-Based Shaper
(CBS, IEEE 802.1Qav) so that it rate-limits traffic generated by a DPDK
Virtual Function (VF) driven by the enetc4_vf PMD.

The procedure is not tied to any single SoC. It applies to any NXP platform
that integrates the NETC/ENETC block and is served by the enetc4_vf DPDK
driver (for example i.MX95, i.MX943, and later parts). The only per-platform /
per-board differences are values you must read from your own system - link
speed, the number of Traffic Classes, and the PCI/interface names - not the
method itself.

This guide was tested on the i.MX95 EVK; the commands and register offsets
shown were verified there.

The important architectural point is that the CBS shaper is NOT a VF resource.
It is a per-Traffic-Class (per-TC) shaper that lives in the ENETC PORT register
space, which is owned by the Physical Function (PF / PSI). A VF (VSI) cannot
program it directly. However, because CBS shapes a whole port TC - and that TC
is shared by all Station Interfaces (the PF and every VF) - a shaper enabled by
the PF on a given TC also throttles the VF traffic that lands in that same TC.

So the test is a hybrid, and it needs NO new driver code:
  - The PF stays bound to the kernel driver (nxp_enetc4 / fsl_enetc4) and
    programs the CBS shaper on a TC using the standard Linux "tc ... cbs"
    utility.
  - The DPDK VF steers its Tx ring into that same TC using the existing PMD
    device argument "enetc4_txq_prior" (which writes the Tx BD ring mode
    register TBaMR[PRIO]).
  - Traffic is generated from the DPDK VF (testpmd txonly) and the egress rate
    is measured to confirm the shaper caps it.


Background: NETC transmit scheduling hierarchy
==============================================
Each ENETC port has a three-level hierarchical transmit scheduler (see the
NETC "Transmit scheduling" section of your platform Reference Manual):

  Level            Selection algorithm                Register        Owner
  ---------------- ---------------------------------- --------------- ------
  ROOT (port, /TC) TSD, CBS (802.1Qav), EST(Qbv),     PTCaCBSR0/1     PF
                   Strict Priority                    (port space)
  MID  (SI/VSI)    WBFS (weighted bandwidth fair)     PSIaCFGR0[SIBW] PF
                                                      (port space)
  LEAF (Tx BDR)    Strict priority + frame-based WRR  TBaMR[PRIO]     VF
                                                      TBaMR[WRR]      (SI space)

- CBS (the shaper) sits at the ROOT node, per Traffic Class. Registers
  PTCaCBSR0 (enable + bandwidth) and PTCaCBSR1 (hiCredit) exist for every TC
  (a = 0..7). CBS bandwidth is expressed as a percentage of the port transmit
  rate (0.0 - 100.0%, in 0.1% units). Hardware allows CBS on up to 7 TCs
  concurrently.
- What decides which TC a VF frame belongs to is the leaf register the VF owns:
  TBaMR[PRIO]. The DPDK devarg "enetc4_txq_prior" writes this field, so it maps
  the VF Tx ring to a port TC.


Traffic-class count and the CBS "top two TCs" rule
==================================================
- The number of TCs is SoC/port configurable (up to 8). ALWAYS read the actual
  count from your platform - do not assume it. Different SoCs and even
  different ports on the same SoC can expose a different number of TCs. In the
  examples below a 6-TC port (TC0..TC5) is used, so the top TC is TC5; adjust
  the TC index to match your board.
- Hardware supports CBS on any TC (up to 7 of them). HOWEVER the Linux kernel
  driver (enetc4_setup_tc_cbs() in drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/enetc/
  enetc4_tc.c) intentionally implements CBS only on the top two TCs, following
  the classic 802.1Qav AVB Class A / Class B model:
        high_prio_tc   = tc_nums - 1;   (e.g. TC5 for 6 TCs)  -> Class A
        second_prio_tc = tc_nums - 2;   (e.g. TC4 for 6 TCs)  -> Class B
  A "tc ... cbs" request on any other TC returns -EOPNOTSUPP.
- The top TC (Class A) must be enabled BEFORE the second TC (Class B), and the
  sum of all CBS bandwidth must be < 100%.

  So when driving CBS via the kernel "tc" utility, use the top TC of your port
  (TC5 on a 6-TC port) - and steer the VF ring to that same TC.


Prerequisites
=============
1. Bind the VF to the uio_pci_generic driver so it can be used from DPDK, and
   keep the PF bound to the kernel driver with its link UP. The PF MUST remain
   on the kernel driver: it services the VF mailbox AND owns the CBS shaper.

        modprobe uio_pci_generic
        dpdk-devbind.py -b uio_pci_generic <vf-pci-addr>
        dpdk-devbind.py -s        # confirm the VF is bound to uio_pci_generic

2. DPDK built for arm64 (see nxp/README) with the testpmd application.

3. Identify (do not assume) the following on your board:
      - PF eth interface (e.g. eth1) and PF link speed (ethtool <pf-eth>)
      - Number of TCs on the port, hence the top TC index
      - VF PCI address <vf-pci-addr> (dpdk-devbind.py -s / dmesg)
   In the examples below the PF is "eth1" linked at 10 Gbps and the port has
   6 TCs (TC0..TC5), so the CBS-eligible top TC is TC5. Substitute the values
   that match your platform.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1 - On the kernel PF: create the TC map and enable CBS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
First confirm the PF link speed - the CBS slope values depend on it:

        ethtool eth1 | grep -E "Speed|Link detected"
        # Example output:
        #   Speed: 10000Mb/s
        #   Link detected: yes

a) Give the port a TC->queue map with mqprio. netdev_get_num_tc() must be > 0
   for the CBS math to work. Here 6 TCs are configured (TC0..TC5); use the TC
   count your port actually exposes:

        tc qdisc add dev eth1 root handle 100: mqprio \
            num_tc 6 map 0 1 2 3 4 5 5 5 queues 1@0 1@1 1@2 1@3 1@4 1@5 hw 1

   Verify:
        tc qdisc show dev eth1
   mqprio classes are 1-based, so the top TC (TC5) is "parent 100:6".

b) Enable CBS on the TOP TC (TC5 = parent 100:6).

   The kernel enforces:  idleslope - sendslope == link_speed_in_kbps
   with idleslope >= 0 and sendslope <= 0. Compute the slopes from the link
   speed and the bandwidth percentage you want to cap the TC to:

        link_speed_kbps = <link Mbps> * 1000
        idleslope       = cap_percent% * link_speed_kbps
        sendslope       = idleslope - link_speed_kbps

   For a 10 Gbps link (link_speed_kbps = 10,000,000), 20% cap on TC5:

        tc qdisc replace dev eth1 parent 100:6 cbs \
            idleslope 2000000 sendslope -8000000 hicredit 153 locredit -1389 offload 1

   10 Gbps slope reference table:

        Cap %   idleslope   sendslope
        -----   ---------   ----------
         10%     1000000     -9000000
         20%     2000000     -8000000
         50%     5000000     -5000000
         90%     9000000     -1000000

   For other link speeds, recompute using the formulas above (e.g. on a 1 Gbps
   link, link_speed_kbps = 1,000,000, so a 20% cap is idleslope 200000 /
   sendslope -800000).

   NOTE:
   - hicredit/locredit passed on the command line are recomputed by the driver
     from idleslope; their exact values do not affect acceptance. Only the
     idleslope/sendslope-vs-link-speed check matters. The numbers shown above
     are just illustrative placeholders; if you prefer, you can omit them
     entirely. For reference, the 802.1Qav definition is
     hicredit = maxFrameSize * idleslope / (linkSpeed - idleslope) and
     locredit = maxFrameSize * sendslope / linkSpeed.
   - The link MUST be up when running this command; if priv->speed is 0 the
     check fails and you get "Specified device failed to setup cbs hardware
     offload".
   - Wrong slope scale is the most common failure: slopes computed for one link
     speed are rejected on a link of a different speed because their difference
     no longer equals the link's kbps.

   Verify:
        tc qdisc show dev eth1     # shows the cbs qdisc under 100:6, offload


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 2 - On the DPDK VF: steer the Tx ring into the CBS TC (TC5)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The PMD devarg "enetc4_txq_prior" writes TBaMR[PRIO] per Tx queue, which maps
the ring to a port TC. To land the VF ring in the shaped TC, set the priority
to that TC index (5 in these examples).

Single Tx queue:

        ./dpdk-testpmd -c 0x3 -a <vf-pci-addr>,enetc4_txq_prior=5 -- -i --portmask=0x1

Multiple Tx queues: pass a "|"-separated priority list in queue order, e.g.
for two queues both in TC5:

        ./dpdk-testpmd -c 0x3 -a <vf-pci-addr>,enetc4_txq_prior="5|5" -- -i --portmask=0x1


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 3 - Generate traffic and measure the shaped rate
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
In testpmd, saturate egress with large frames and read the Tx rate:

        testpmd> set fwd txonly
        testpmd> set txpkts 1518
        testpmd> start
        ... let it run a few seconds ...
        testpmd> show port stats 0

Read Tx-bps. With a 20% cap on a 10G port it should settle around ~2 Gbps
(minus framing overhead), not near line rate. Scale your expectation to your
own link speed and cap percentage.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 4 - Baseline comparison (CBS off)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Disable or remove the shaper and repeat the measurement:

        # disable offload but keep the qdisc:
        tc qdisc replace dev eth1 parent 100:6 cbs \
            idleslope 2000000 sendslope -8000000 hicredit 153 locredit -1389 offload 0
        # or remove it entirely:
        tc qdisc del dev eth1 parent 100:6 cbs

Re-run in testpmd:

        testpmd> stop
        testpmd> start
        testpmd> show port stats 0

Tx-bps should now be much higher (limited by the DPDK Tx path, not CBS).

Try a couple of CBS bandwidth values (e.g. 10% and 50%) and confirm the
measured Tx-bps tracks the setting - this demonstrates the shaper is effective.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 5 - Teardown
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        tc qdisc del dev eth1 parent 100:6 cbs
        tc qdisc del dev eth1 root


Verifying the VF-ring-to-TC mapping (debug)
===========================================
For the CBS on TC5 to cap the VF traffic, the VF Tx ring must resolve to port
TC5. We set enetc4_txq_prior=5 (writes TBaMR[PRIO]=5). If shaping does not
visibly cap the VF traffic:

  - Confirm the VF ring priority took effect and the IPV->TC mapping is 1:1
    (the kernel mqprio path leaves IPV->TC at its default 1:1, so PRIO 5 -> TC5).
  - Confirm CBS is enabled on the shaped TC. The CBS register for TC "a" is
    PTCaCBSR0 at port offset (a * 0x20 + 0x4210); for TC5 that is
    5 * 0x20 + 0x4210 = 0x42B0. Bit 31 (CBSE) must be set and the low 7 bits
    (BW) hold the bandwidth in units of 1%.
  - The VF ring mode register is TBaMR (here "a" is the Tx BD ring index, not
    the TC index). For ring "n" the SI offset is (n * 0x200 + 0x8000); its
    PRIO field is bits [2:0].


Troubleshooting
===============
- "Specified device failed to setup cbs hardware offload":
    * Link is down or priv->speed is 0 - bring the PF link up first.
    * idleslope/sendslope do not match the link speed. The kernel requires
      idleslope - sendslope == link_speed_kbps (e.g. 10,000,000 for 10G).
      Recompute the slopes from the actual "ethtool <pf-eth>" Speed.
    * CBS requested on a non-top-two TC. The kernel only supports CBS on the
      top TC and the one below it. Use the top TC of your port.
    * For the second TC, the top TC must be enabled first, and the sum of CBS
      bandwidth must stay below 100%.

- Shaper enabled but VF traffic not capped:
    * The VF ring is not in the shaped TC. Ensure enetc4_txq_prior matches the
      CBS TC index and that IPV->TC mapping is 1:1.


Notes
=====
- This procedure is platform-independent: it works on any NXP SoC whose NETC/
  ENETC block is served by the enetc4_vf PMD (i.MX95, i.MX943, etc.). Only the
  per-board values (link speed, TC count, PCI/interface names) change.
- No DPDK driver changes are required for this test: the shaper is programmed
  by the kernel PF and the VF only needs the existing enetc4_txq_prior devarg.
- The "top two TCs" restriction is a Linux enetc driver software policy
  (enetc4_setup_tc_cbs), not a hardware limit; the hardware provides a CBS
  register (PTCaCBSR0/1) for every TC and supports CBS on up to 7 TCs.
- The PF must remain bound to the kernel driver and be link-up throughout: it
  both services the VF (VSI) mailbox and owns the port CBS shaper.
